[/tikz]

workedExamples.solution_text:
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}
\definecolor{curveBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\begin{axis}[width=9cm,height=5.5cm,axis lines=middle,axis line style={black,->},xlabel={$x$},ylabel={$y$},label style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},tick label style={font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont,text=black},xmin=-3.4,xmax=3.4,ymin=-3.5,ymax=3.5,xtick={-3.141593,-1.570796,0,1.570796,3.141593},xticklabels={$-\pi$,$-\frac{\pi}{2}$,$0$,$\frac{\pi}{2}$,$\pi$},ytick={-1,1},clip=true]
\addplot[black,dashed] coordinates {(-1.570796,-3.5) (-1.570796,3.5)};
\addplot[black,dashed] coordinates {(1.570796,-3.5) (1.570796,3.5)};
\addplot[curveBlue,thick,samples=101,domain=-3.141593:-1.849096,->] {tan(deg(x))};
\addplot[curveBlue,thick,samples=151,domain=-1.292496:1.292496,<->] {tan(deg(x))};
\addplot[curveBlue,thick,samples=101,domain=1.849096:3.141593,<-] {tan(deg(x))};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The intercepts are $(-\pi,0)$, $(0,0)$ and $(\pi,0)$. The vertical asymptotes are $x=-\frac{\pi}{2}$ and $x=\frac{\pi}{2}$.

Since $\tan x=\frac{\sin x}{\cos x}$, tangent is undefined where $\cos x=0$.

Unrestricted domain: $\left\{x\in\mathbb R:x\ne\frac{\pi}{2}+k\pi,\ k\in\mathbb Z\right\}$. Range: $(-\infty,\infty)$.

Each branch repeats after $\pi$, so the period is $\pi$. Tangent has no amplitude.

Since $\tan(-x)=-\tan x$, tangent is odd and its graph has rotational symmetry of $180^\circ$ about the origin.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}
\definecolor{curveBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{levelRed}{HTML}{EF6068}
\begin{axis}[width=10cm,height=5cm,axis lines=middle,axis line style={black,->},xlabel={$x$},ylabel={$y$},label style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},tick label style={font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont,text=black},xmin=-0.2,xmax=6.6,ymin=-1.4,ymax=1.4,xtick={0,1.570796,3.141593,4.712389,6.283185},xticklabels={$0$,$\frac{\pi}{2}$,$\pi$,$\frac{3\pi}{2}$,$2\pi$},ytick={-1,-0.5,1},yticklabels={$-1$,$-\frac12$,$1$},clip=true]
\addplot[curveBlue,thick,samples=201,domain=0:6.283185] {sin(deg(x))};
\addplot[levelRed,dashed] coordinates {(0,-0.5) (6.283185,-0.5)};
\addplot[black,only marks,mark=*] coordinates {(3.665191,-0.5) (5.759587,-0.5)};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The sine curve passes through $(0,0)$, $\left(\frac{\pi}{2},1\right)$, $(\pi,0)$, $\left(\frac{3\pi}{2},-1\right)$ and $(2\pi,0)$.

The horizontal line $y=-\frac12$ intersects the curve twice, in Quadrants 3 and 4. The reference angle is $\frac{\pi}{6}$.

$\begin{aligned}x&=\pi+\frac{\pi}{6}=\frac{7\pi}{6}\end{aligned}$

$\begin{aligned}x&=2\pi-\frac{\pi}{6}=\frac{11\pi}{6}\end{aligned}$

Sine is odd, so reflecting a point through the origin changes the sign of its value.

$\begin{aligned}\sin\left(-\frac{\pi}{3}\right)&=-\sin\frac{\pi}{3}\\&=-\frac{\sqrt3}{2}\end{aligned}$

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}
\definecolor{sineBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{cosineRed}{HTML}{EF6068}
\begin{axis}[width=10cm,height=5cm,axis lines=middle,axis line style={black,->},xlabel={$x$},ylabel={$y$},label style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},tick label style={font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont,text=black},legend style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,draw=none,at={(0.5,1.15)},anchor=south,legend columns=2},xmin=-3.4,xmax=3.4,ymin=-1.4,ymax=1.4,xtick={-3.141593,-1.570796,0,1.570796,3.141593},xticklabels={$-\pi$,$-\frac{\pi}{2}$,$0$,$\frac{\pi}{2}$,$\pi$},ytick={-1,1},clip=true]
\addplot[sineBlue,thick,samples=201,domain=-3.141593:3.141593] {sin(deg(x))};
\addlegendentry{$y=\sin x$}
\addplot[cosineRed,thick,dashed,samples=201,domain=-3.141593:3.141593] {cos(deg(x))};
\addlegendentry{$y=\cos x$}
\addplot[black,only marks,mark=*,forget plot] coordinates {(-2.356194,-0.707107) (0.785398,0.707107)};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The curves intersect once between $-\pi$ and $-\frac{\pi}{2}$ and once between $0$ and $\frac{\pi}{2}$.

At the reference angle $\frac{\pi}{4}$, sine and cosine both equal $\frac1{\sqrt2}$. In Quadrant 3, both equal $-\frac1{\sqrt2}$.

The solutions are $x=-\frac{3\pi}{4}$ and $x=\frac{\pi}{4}$.

The intersection coordinates are $\left(-\frac{3\pi}{4},-\frac1{\sqrt2}\right)$ and $\left(\frac{\pi}{4},\frac1{\sqrt2}\right)$.

foundation.question_text:
A trigonometric graph is shown below.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\begin{axis}[axis lines=middle, xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$},
  xmin=-0.3, xmax=6.8, ymin=-4, ymax=4,
  xtick={1.5708, 3.1416, 4.7124, 6.2832},
  xticklabels={$\frac{\pi}{2}$, $\pi$, $\frac{3\pi}{2}$, $2\pi$},
  ytick={-3,-2,-1,1,2,3},
  samples=200, clip=false,
  restrict y to domain=-4:4, unbounded coords=jump,
  axis line style={-{Stealth}}, width=11cm, height=6.5cm]
\addplot[smooth, thick, blue, domain=0.01:1.45] {tan(deg(x))};
\addplot[smooth, thick, blue, domain=1.69:4.59] {tan(deg(x))};
\addplot[smooth, thick, blue, domain=4.83:6.27] {tan(deg(x))};
\addplot[dashed, gray, domain=-4:4] ({1.5708},{x});
\addplot[dashed, gray, domain=-4:4] ({4.7124},{x});
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Identify which trigonometric function is shown: $y = \sin x$, $y = \cos x$, or $y = \tan x$.

mastery.question_text:
The graphs of $y = \sin x$ and $y = \cos x$ are plotted together on $[0, 2\pi]$.
[tikz]\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\begin{axis}[axis lines=middle, xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$},
  xmin=-0.3, xmax=6.8, ymin=-1.4, ymax=1.4,
  xtick={1.5708, 3.1416, 4.7124, 6.2832},
  xticklabels={$\frac{\pi}{2}$, $\pi$, $\frac{3\pi}{2}$, $2\pi$},
  ytick={-1, 1},
  samples=200, clip=false,
  axis line style={-{Stealth}}, width=11cm, height=6.5cm]
\addplot[smooth, thick, blue, domain=0:6.2832] {sin(deg(x))};
\addplot[smooth, thick, red, dashed, domain=0:6.2832] {cos(deg(x))};
\node[blue, above right] at (axis cs:2.6,0.5) {$y=\sin x$};
\node[red, below left] at (axis cs:2.0944,-0.5) {$y=\cos x$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}[/tikz]
Find all values of $x$ in $[0, 2\pi]$ where $\sin x = \cos x$, and state the coordinates of the intersection points.
